    "key": "post:921611111039858_122141469279200344",
    "link": "https://www.facebook.com/122141783835200344/posts/122141469279200344",
    "origin": "auto",
    "page_id": "921611111039858",
    "text": "My 12-year-old son stepped onto the school stage to sing for the father he had lost three years earlier.\n\nCaleb had changed after David died.\n\nHe stopped inviting friends over, often ate alone in his room, and whenever I asked what was wrong, he gave me the same answer:\n\n“I’m fine.”\n\nThat was why I had been nervous about the school’s Father’s Day event.\n\nI even considered keeping him home.\n\nBut Caleb insisted on going.\n\nThen his name was called.\n\nHe walked onto the stage alone, carrying the old acoustic guitar David had taught him to play.\n\nI had no idea he had prepared anything.\n\nCaleb searched the audience until he found me.\n\nThen he looked upward.\n\n“This one is for my dad.”\n\nThe first notes nearly broke me.\n\nIt was the same song David used to hum in our kitchen on Saturday mornings.\n\nBy the second verse, several parents were wiping their eyes.\n\nEven the teachers along the wall had gone completely still.\n\nThen, without warning, the doors at the back of the gym opened.\n\nTwo uniformed police officers walked inside.\n\nOne of them looked toward Caleb.\n\nThen he slowly removed his hat.\n\nAnd when my son saw what the officer was holding in his other hand, he suddenly stopped singing.\n\nContinue reading in the comments👇"
   }
